"# Cross-Domain Evaluation of CNNs, Vision Transformers, and Vision Mamba\n\n![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/Status-Completed-success)\n![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.x-blue)\n![PyTorch](https://img.shields.io/badge/PyTorch-Deep%20Learning-red)\n![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green)\n\nA comparative evaluation of modern deep learning architectures across multiple visual domains, focusing on classification accuracy, confidence calibration, and robustness under image corruption.\n\n---\n\n## Overview\n\nDeep learning models are commonly evaluated using classification accuracy. However, models with similar accuracy can behave very differently in terms of confidence calibration and robustness when deployed in real-world environments.\n\nThis project presents a comparative evaluation of three vision architectures—**ResNet-18**, **Vision Transformer (ViT-Small)**, and **Vision Mamba**—across three distinct image domains:\n\n- Natural Images (CIFAR-100)\n- Satellite Imagery (EuroSAT)\n- Medical Imaging (Brain MRI)\n\nA unified experimental pipeline was designed to ensure fair and reproducible comparison across all datasets and model architectures. In addition to classification performance, the study evaluates model reliability through confidence calibration and robustness under image corruption.\n\n---\n\n## Project Highlights\n\n- Cross-domain evaluation across three visual domains\n- Unified experimental pipeline\n- Comparison of CNN, Transformer, and Sequential Vision architectures\n- Multi-seed experiments for improved reproducibility\n- Confidence calibration using ECE and MCE\n- Temperature Scaling analysis\n- Robustness evaluation using Gaussian Noise and Gaussian Blur\n\n---\n\n## Objectives\n\n- Compare CNN, Transformer, and Sequential Vision models across multiple datasets.\n- Evaluate confidence calibration alongside classification accuracy.\n- Measure model robustness under image corruption.\n- Study model behaviour across different visual domains.\n- Build a reproducible evaluation pipeline for fair model comparison.\n\n---\n\n## Datasets\n\n| Dataset | Domain | Classification Task |\n|---------|--------|---------------------|\n| CIFAR-100 | Natural Images | 100-Class Classification |\n| EuroSAT | Satellite Imagery | 10-Class Classification |\n| Brain MRI | Medical Imaging | Binary Classification |\n\n---\n\n## Model Architectures\n\n- **ResNet-18 (CNN)**\n- **Vision Transformer (ViT-Small)**\n- **Vision Mamba**\n\n---\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\n- Accuracy\n- F1 Score\n- Expected Calibration Error (ECE)\n- Maximum Calibration Error (MCE)\n- Temperature Scaling\n- Robustness under Gaussian Noise\n- Robustness under Gaussian Blur\n\n---\n\n## Experimental Pipeline\n\n```text\nDatasets\n    │\n    ▼\nData Preprocessing\n    │\n    ▼\nModel Training\n    │\n    ▼\nPerformance Evaluation\n    │\n    ▼\nCalibration Analysis\n    │\n    ▼\nRobustness Testing\n    │\n    ▼\nResult Analysis\n```\n\n---\n\n## Repository Structure\n\n```text\ncross-domain-vision-model-evaluation/\n\n├── assets/\n├── datasets/\n├── docs/\n├── evaluation/\n├── models/\n├── notebooks/\n├── references/\n├── results/\n├── training/\n├── LICENSE\n├── README.md\n└── requirements.txt\n```\n\n---\n\n## Repository Status\n\n🚧 **This repository is currently being organized.**\n\nThe project report and documentation are available. The implementation code will be uploaded after consolidating the final project source files.\n\n---\n\n## Results\n\nThe study demonstrates that model selection should not rely solely on classification accuracy.\n\nSome key observations include:\n\n- Vision Transformer (ViT-Small) achieved the strongest overall performance on the CIFAR-100 dataset.\n- ResNet-18 remained highly competitive on the Brain MRI dataset.\n- Models with similar accuracy often exhibited different calibration and robustness characteristics.\n- Temperature Scaling improved calibration for some models but was not universally beneficial.\n- Robustness varied considerably across architectures under Gaussian Noise and Gaussian Blur.\n\nDetailed experimental results and visualizations will be added to this repository.\n\n---\n\n## My Contributions\n\nMy primary contributions to this project include:\n\n- Designing the unified evaluation pipeline\n- Implementing evaluation metrics\n- Calibration analysis using Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and Maximum Calibration Error (MCE)\n- Temperature Scaling experiments\n- Robustness evaluation using Gaussian Noise and Gaussian Blur\n- Multi-seed reproducibility\n- Testing, verification, and experimental result analysis\n\n---\n\n## Future Work\n\nPotential extensions of this project include:\n\n- Using pretrained Vision Mamba models for fairer comparison\n- Evaluating additional image corruptions such as motion blur, JPEG compression, and occlusion\n- Investigating advanced calibration techniques beyond Temperature Scaling\n- Performing cross-domain transfer learning experiments\n- Expanding evaluation to larger medical and remote sensing datasets\n\n---\n\n## References\n\nThis project is based on concepts and methodologies introduced in the following works:\n\n- Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition (ResNet)\n- An Image is Worth 16×16 Words: Transformers for Image Recognition at Scale (ViT)\n- Vision Mamba\n- On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks\n- Revisiting the Calibration of Modern Neural Networks\n\nA complete reference list is available in the project report.\n\n---\n\n## Contributors\n\nThis project was completed as the Final Year B.Tech (Computer Science & Engineering) project at **KIIT Deemed to be University**.\n\n- Shirish Baral\n- Pratik Timilsina\n- Madan Pandey\n- Genish Kumar\n- Sujit Pangeni\n\n---\n\n## Supervisor\n\n**Nayan Kumar Subhashis Behera**  \nAssistant Professor  \nSchool of Computer Engineering  \nKIIT Deemed to be University\n\n---\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nThe authors sincerely thank **Nayan Kumar Subhashis Behera** for his guidance throughout the project. The experiments were conducted using the Kaggle platform and various open-source deep learning libraries, which made the implementation and evaluation possible.\n"